  "heat": 3,
  "text": "He asked for water. She gave him milk. Then she waited for him to fall asleep. Sisera. Nine hundred chariots of iron. Twenty years crushing Israel. Now he is on foot. Alone. His army just collapsed. He runs for the nearest tent. A woman named Jael. Her husband's people are at peace with his king. He thinks it is safe. She comes out to meet him. And says: \"Turn in, my lord, turn in to me. Fear not.\" Fear not. Remember that. He asks for a little water. She opens a bottle of milk. Covers him up. Then he gives her orders. Stand in the door. Anybody asks if there is a man in here, say no. \"For he was fast asleep and weary.\" Then: \"Jael Heber's wife took a nail of the tent, and took an hammer in her hand, and went softly unto him.\" Went softly. Then she walks out, finds the general hunting him, and says, \"Come, and I will shew thee the man whom thou seekest.\" The audacity. And Deborah puts it in a song. Blessed above women, she calls her. But here is the part nobody tells you. The same song cuts to Sisera's mother. At a window. Waiting. \"Why is his chariot so long in coming?\" Her ladies reassure her. He is late because he is dividing the spoils. \"To every man a damsel or two.\" That is in the victory song. The last thing you hear about her. Scripture does not flinch. So I will not.",
